                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Looking for a solid overall - all around crafting, exploring, automating, base building type game can be hard to find. Scrap Mechanic seemed to scratch that itch with the addition of multiplayer, but I should warn Gamers that isn't the run of the mill type. The learning curve is quite steep and the game will require thinking (and researching) to figure out the mechanics. If you got time - pretty good game, if you don't - small strides may ruin the experience.\n\nScrap Mechanics [b]gameplay[/b] is pretty solid all around. Movement, craft mechanics, building... the entire environment can't be manipulated, but quite a bit can be picked up and moved. The game starts off with a choice of three modes: creative, survival, and challenge - survival being the \"default\" game mode. The player is plopped into a world that generates a random map seed. Each seed contains specific buildings core to the game's story. Those don't change much, the environment around does. Throughout the map are resources that sometimes are available naturally throughout the map or need to be acquired by exploring certain areas of interest (like scrap towers... which seem to be everywhere). Some items require direct combat with rogue robots to salvage their parts to craft new items. The [b]combat[/b] mechanics are fun and bouncy. The weapons do feel different and I feel the devs tried to vary each weapons approach a bit. Learning the game though requires quite some time. The player is dropped into the world with waypoints and is sort of expected to learn along the way. I felt the difficulty sort of jumped as I required a little more research and reading into how bearings, wheels, shocks, engines, buttons, vacuums, and how a lot of other items work. Figured okay - would try to plant a chill garden try to achieve the tomato challenge for the farmer and I was getting raided by robots. Was a surprise, cool feature, but can be difficult first time around with absolutely zero defence structures made. Interesting world, can be hard to learn depending on what wants to be done.\n\nThe [b]graphics and visuals[/b] were pretty well done. The game has its own identity and assets flow well with one another. Watching the character bounce, while driving \"a vehicle\", in the most redneck lawn chair go-kart setup I've ever seen is pretty cool, as the wind moves the bushes and branches around the world. Animations overall we're well done (as they seem to be almost everywhere) and really add to the [b]gameplay[/b] experience. Hammering trees to get wood and watching them fall over was pretty cool. Not Valheim levels of tree chopping, but decently cool. The [b]sounds and music[/b] were another domain done well. Music was pretty chill throughout and sounds weren't repetitive enough to be annoying - which is always a huge bonus in crafting games. \n\nOverall, the game is good, its nice, but it is one of those games that will definitely need a little extra attention, research, and reading. I've played Satisfactory, Factorio, Dyson Sphere, Hydroneer few others of the kind and I thought I'd be ready to build a car and get it running in no time - yeah it took a bit of figuring out. Same with pumps, vacuums, irrigation systems, farming systems, and yeah... there's still stuff I'm figuring out. I will recommend it but with caution and to buy it on sale. It's a good game, lot to offer, and the fact it's multiplayer makes it all the better. However, it will require some patience! What sort of ruined the game for me was this giant thing (Seekerbot?) floating in the air which would essentially attack the player or anything the player built. At first it was cool, but became annoying pretty quick. Not fun being bombarded all the time!\n\n\n[h1]RATING: 8.0/10[/h1]\n\n\n[table]\n[tr]\n[td]Gameplay[/td]\n[td]B[/td]\n[/tr]\n[tr]\n[td]Story / Campaign[/td]\n[td]B-[/td]\n[/tr]\n[tr]\n[td]Visuals / User Interface[/td]\n[td]B+[/td]\n[/tr]\n[tr]\n[td]Sounds / Music[/td]\n[td]B+[/td]\n[/tr]\n[tr]\n[td]Replay-ability[/td]\n[td]B[/td]\n[/tr]\n[tr]\n[td]Overall [/td]\n[td]B-[/td]\n[/tr]\n[/table]",
